WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.411
Picture a takeaway counter.

2
00:00:04.411 --> 00:00:09.799
You order a burger, pay, and the cashier hands you a ticket with the number 42 on it.

3
00:00:09.799 --> 00:00:11.637
The burger doesn't exist yet.

4
00:00:11.637 --> 00:00:15.313
The ticket promises that it will, and lets you collect it.

5
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:18.101
With the ticket, you can do three things.

6
00:00:18.101 --> 00:00:20.543
Wait at the counter until 42 is called.

7
00:00:20.543 --> 00:00:23.736
Wait only five minutes, because your bus is coming.

8
00:00:23.736 --> 00:00:26.867
Or glance at the screen to see whether it's ready.

9
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:29.543
A thread pool hands out the same ticket.

10
00:00:29.543 --> 00:00:36.476
You give it a task with submit, and you get a Future back at once, a promise of a result that doesn't exist yet.

11
00:00:36.476 --> 00:00:38.395
get is the wait at the counter.

12
00:00:38.395 --> 00:00:41.923
get with a time limit is the five minutes before the bus.

13
00:00:41.923 --> 00:00:44.337
And isDone is the glance at the screen.

14
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:48.668
Now say you give up after five minutes and catch your bus.

15
00:00:48.668 --> 00:00:51.090
Here's a question most of us never ask.

16
00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.767
What happens to the burger?

17
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:57.262
Nobody tells the kitchen.

18
00:00:57.262 --> 00:00:59.161
At minute seven, it's ready.

19
00:00:59.161 --> 00:01:01.603
Hot, paid for, and nobody to eat it.

20
00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:05.129
Somewhere, a burger is having a very lonely evening.

21
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:08.038
Java behaves the same way.

22
00:01:08.038 --> 00:01:15.080
The lesson's TimeoutDemo starts a task that takes 600 milliseconds, and waits only 200.

23
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.832
main gives up at 285 milliseconds.

24
00:01:17.832 --> 00:01:21.960
The task never hears about it, and finishes at 771.

25
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:25.844
So the point is, a time limit ends your wait, not the work.

26
00:01:25.844 --> 00:01:28.836
If the work should stop, somebody has to say so.

27
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:32.472
A Future has a method for that, cancel(true).

28
00:01:32.472 --> 00:01:34.966
So here's the question we're shy to ask.

29
00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:36.961
Does cancel(true) kill the task?

30
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:40.459
It can't.

31
00:01:40.459 --> 00:01:44.570
Java has no safe way to kill a thread from outside.

32
00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:50.697
The old Thread.stop was marked don't use for decades, and JDK 26 removed it.

33
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.333
Here's what really happens.

34
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:58.402
Picture the manager walking over to a cook's station and sticking a note on it.

35
00:01:58.402 --> 00:01:59.750
Please stop order 42.

36
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.547
Then the manager walks away.

37
00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:02.602
Let's map it.

38
00:02:02.602 --> 00:02:05.559
The cook is the pool thread running your task.

39
00:02:05.559 --> 00:02:10.188
The note is an interrupt, a request to a thread to stop what it's doing.

40
00:02:10.188 --> 00:02:16.166
And the note on the station is the interrupt flag, a true or false value inside every thread.

41
00:02:16.166 --> 00:02:19.059
cancel(true) sets it to true, and that's all.

42
00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:22.411
Whether a note works depends on the cook.

43
00:02:22.411 --> 00:02:23.776
Our kitchen has three.

44
00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:25.886
The first is waiting for the oven.

45
00:02:25.886 --> 00:02:29.547
The second is chopping onions, and looks up after each one.

46
00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:32.401
The third is chopping too, and never looks up.

47
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:35.435
The lesson's CancelDemo builds all three.

48
00:02:35.435 --> 00:02:37.758
The first sleeps for five seconds.

49
00:02:37.758 --> 00:02:43.225
The second does 400 slices of arithmetic, and checks the flag before each slice.

50
00:02:43.225 --> 00:02:46.847
The third does the same 400 slices, and never checks.

51
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:51.607
After 100 milliseconds, main calls cancel(true) on all three.

52
00:02:51.607 --> 00:02:52.797
Which ones stop?

53
00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.805
The oven cook stops at once.

54
00:02:57.805 --> 00:03:04.794
A thread that waits, in sleep or on a queue, is woken by the note, with an InterruptedException.

55
00:03:04.794 --> 00:03:10.763
The checker sees the note the next time it looks up, after 51 slices, and returns.

56
00:03:10.763 --> 00:03:17.169
The third cook chops on, all 400 slices, and finishes 769 milliseconds after the cancel.

57
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.978
Focus is a wonderful quality in a cook, and a terrible one in a task you're trying to stop.

58
00:03:22.978 --> 00:03:26.288
The note only works on a cook who waits, or who looks.

59
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.097
Now glance at the tickets.

60
00:03:28.097 --> 00:03:31.658
Right after the cancel, all three said cancelled and done.

61
00:03:31.658 --> 00:03:34.974
Even the third one, while its cook was still chopping.

62
00:03:34.974 --> 00:03:38.290
A Future changes its state the moment you call cancel.

63
00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.562
It doesn't wait for the task to stop.

64
00:03:40.562 --> 00:03:44.737
So that burger gets made anyway, under a ticket that says cancelled.

65
00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:48.230
And here the kitchen picture breaks, twice.

66
00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:54.014
A real cook hears you shout across the room, but a thread hears nothing unless it waits or looks.

67
00:03:54.014 --> 00:03:57.054
And a real kitchen knows whether the dish was made.

68
00:03:57.054 --> 00:03:58.664
A cancelled Future doesn't.

69
00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:02.053
This is called cooperative cancellation.

70
00:04:02.053 --> 00:04:04.122
It works only if the task helps.

71
00:04:04.122 --> 00:04:07.679
If I could rename cancel(true), I'd call it pleaseStop.

72
00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:12.350
That's why the cancel run of TimeoutDemo worked.

73
00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:16.979
main gave up at 257 milliseconds, and the task stopped at 260.

74
00:04:16.979 --> 00:04:20.264
Its cook was waiting for the oven, in sleep.

75
00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:25.559
So a long computing loop should look up now and then, like the checker.

76
00:04:25.559 --> 00:04:29.228
In Java and Kotlin, it's one check at the top of the loop.

77
00:04:29.228 --> 00:04:30.936
Is this thread interrupted?

78
00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:31.569
Then stop.

79
00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:34.732
Calls that wait, like sleep, already look for you.

80
00:04:35.533 --> 00:04:37.403
So, does cancel stop a task?

81
00:04:37.403 --> 00:04:37.937
It asks.

82
00:04:37.937 --> 00:04:39.806
A time limit ends your wait.

83
00:04:39.806 --> 00:04:42.076
cancel puts a note on the station.

84
00:04:42.076 --> 00:04:47.684
Only a cook who waits or looks up will read it, and the ticket can't tell you which.

85
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.670
Quick check.

86
00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:53.992
A task runs a computing loop that never checks the flag, and you call cancel(true).

87
00:04:53.992 --> 00:04:57.712
What does isCancelled return, and when does the task stop?

88
00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:01.646
True, at once.

89
00:05:01.646 --> 00:05:04.553
And the task stops only when its loop ends.
